 available and unused.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Timeline, count, and both cited issues check out exactly as the row states. The row is the item's strongest evidence instance and it holds without qualification.\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: git commits fa68daa08, 6034d1288 (2026-09-15); /Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/.codex/hooks.json:2-50; issues #3446, #3437 (both added 2026-09-06)\\n  VERIFIED_BY: git + Read + backlog_view on 2026-09-15\\n\\n---\\n\\n### C2b \u2014 Evidence table row (2026-09-15): the 63 `relevance_unanchored` entries\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"One research entry tripped `relevance_unanchored` | that entry, by adding an anchor to it | the 63 entries carrying the same warning, and the anchor being enforced at review rather than at write\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED on the 63; INCONCLUSIVE on the single-entry fix\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: `uv run python .claude/skills/research-curator/scripts/validate_research.py main --json research`\\n- Retrieved: 2026-09-15\\n- Content: |\\n    total_entries: 331\\n    entries_with_relevance_unanchored: 63\\n    (raw issue objects with check==\\\"relevance_unanchored\\\": 63 \u2014 one per entry, no entry double-counted)\\n  Exact match to the claimed 63. The check itself is declared at `.claude/skills/research-curator/scripts/validate_research.py:579`, with a date-based exemption cutoff documented at line 99.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: `git log --all --since=2026-09-14 -- research/`, per-commit file counts\\n- Finding: four commits touched `research/` on 2026-09-15 \u2014 a99a84649 (9 files), 9160296a9 (7), dda9ec871 (3), fc4a75883 (7). **None modified exactly one research entry**, and the two anchor-named commits (a99a84649, fc4a75883) concern removed-call citations and unresolvable path citations, not `relevance_unanchored`. The single-entry fix the row describes has no committed artifact.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The denominator is exact and the \\\"enforced at review rather than at write\\\" characterisation is consistent with the check living in a validator script rather than in an authoring path. The narrated one-entry fix may have occurred uncommitted or in a worktree; nothing in committed history corroborates or contradicts it, so it is unresolvable from the repository.\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: .claude/skills/research-curator/scripts/validate_research.py:99,579 \u2014 run against research/ (331 entries) on 2026-09-15; git log --all --since=2026-09-14 -- research/\\n  VERIFIED_BY: CLI (uv run) + git on 2026-09-15\\n\\n---\\n\\n### C2c \u2014 Evidence table row (2026-09-15): the inverted security-upgrade bullet\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"A security-upgrade bullet read as an endorsement after a positive-framing rewrite inverted it | the bullet, reworded in place | the writing pass that can invert a prohibition, which has no gate re-checking the rewrite against its source\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED, including that the fix was made in place at the bullet\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: `git show fa68daa08^:AGENTS.md`, line 385 (the rewrite's source text)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-09-15\\n- Content: |\\n    6. **Dependency security upgrades**: use `uv add \\\"pkg>=X.Y.Z\\\"` (...) rather than\\n       `uv lock --upgrade-package pkg` (silent) ...\\n  The rewrite, `git show fa68daa08 -- rules/astral-tool-overrides.md`, replaced \\\"rather than\\\" with equivalence:\\n    + `uv lock --upgrade-package pkg` does the same silently.\\n  The prohibition became an endorsement of the banned command.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: `git show 6034d1288 -- rules/astral-tool-overrides.md`\\n- Finding: the correction edits that one bullet and nothing else \u2014 `--upgrade-package pkg` moves only the lockfile ... so the vulnerable floor survives`. No gate, test, or check was added in the same commit. The commit message names the inversion in its own words: \\\"the security-upgrade bullet inverted its source ... the rewrite made the two equivalent.\\\"\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Source text, inverted text, and in-place-only repair are all in committed history one hour apart. Both halves of the row \u2014 the inversion and the layer at which it was fixed \u2014 are directly observable.\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: AGENTS.md:385 at fa68daa08^; rules/astral-tool-overrides.md at fa68daa08 and 6034d1288 (2026-09-15)\\n  VERIFIED_BY: git on 2026-09-15\\n\\n---\\n\\n### C2d \u2014 The three original evidence rows (undated entry)\\n\\nCLAIM: The first table's three rows \u2014 duplicate check / SubagentStop hook / prose label absent from every item\\nVERDICT: Row 3 VERIFIED (it is C1a/C1b); rows 1-2 CORROBORATED STRUCTURALLY, not as session events\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: `backlog_core/search.py:580,592`; `backlog_core/operations.py:1407,1430,1477`\\n- Retrieved: 2026-09-15\\n- Content: |\\n    search.py:580  \\\"\\\"\\\"Return casefolded, deduplicated, stopword-filtered words from ``text``, in order.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    search.py:592  \\\"\\\"\\\"Build an OR search query from the significant words in title and description.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    operations.py:1477  def _check_for_duplicates(title, description, force, repo, out) -> None\\n  Term-selection code exists exactly where row 1 says the work was done, and an OR query over stopword-filtered words is a mechanism that returns unrelated matches by construction.\\n- Source: issue #3431 (as filed 2026-09-06T04:50:31Z)\\n- Content: |\\n    One distinct sessionId (the parent's). Thirteen distinct agentId values, one per sub-agent,\\n    matching each transcript's filename stem.\\n  That is row 2's \\\"harness fact that transcripts are per-agent\\\", recorded as a measured reproduction in a separate item.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: `git log --all --since=2026-09-14` and the item's own \u00a7 Context (`session melodic-plotting-emerson, 2026-09-06`)\\n- Finding: the undated entry is a retrospective of a session that left no per-row commit trail, and the table appears twice in the section as duplicate entries (`0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0` and `-1`) with byte-identical content.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The code and harness facts the rows attribute layers to are real and at the stated locations. That a specific session fixed each one at the wrong layer is a claim about session history with no artifact to check it against \u2014 the rows are reportable as design evidence, not as reproducible events. Duplicate entry `-1` is a storage artefact worth striking during grooming.\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/search.py:580,592; backlog_core/operations.py:1407,1430,1477; issue #3431 creation revision\\n  VERIFIED_BY: rg + gh api graphql on 2026-09-15\\n\\n---\\n\\n### C3 \u2014 The layer taxonomy matches how this repo is structured\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"A plugin is code, and instructions as prose that sometimes call the tools that code exposes, and both feed a harness whose hooks, models and tool-calling semantics are also known.\\\" (\u00a7 Description; restated in \u00a7 Scope Note as the repo owner's framing)\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED \u2014 all three layers are separately present, and the prose-calls-the-code relationship is literal\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: file inspection under `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/`\\n- Retrieved: 2026-09-15\\n- Content: |\\n    CODE      backlog_core/server.py:1237,1271,1337,1605 \u2014 @mcp.tool declarations\\n              (backlog_core/ and sam_schema/ are the plugin's two MCP servers)\\n    PROSE     skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finalize.md:269,308,328 \u2014\\n              mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom(selector=..., section=..., mark_groomed=True)\\n              i.e. markdown instructions that invoke the tools the code above exposes\\n    HARNESS   hooks/hooks.json declares PostToolUse, SessionEnd, SessionStart, SubagentStop,\\n              TaskCompleted, UserPromptSubmit, backed by 7 .cjs scripts in hooks/\\n              .claude-plugin/ .codex-plugin/ .cursor-plugin/ \u2014 three per-harness manifests\\n              /Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/.codex/hooks.json \u2014 repo-level Codex events\\n              /Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/harness_compatibility.json \u2014 per-plugin tracker\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: `grep -rn '\\\"hooks\\\"' plugins/development-harness/.claude-plugin .codex-plugin .cursor-plugin`\\n- Finding: no output. The harness layer is not merely present but independently varying \u2014 the plugin ships three harness manifests, none of which declares hooks, which is precisely the packaging gap #3446 records. A defect can therefore sit in the manifest layer while both code and prose are correct, which is what the taxonomy asserts.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Each of the three named layers resolves to distinct files with distinct change mechanics, and the middle layer's defining property \u2014 prose that calls the code's tools \u2014 is verifiable as literal MCP tool-call syntax inside skill markdown. AGENTS.md's own \\\"Skill, Command, and Agent Usage Policy\\\" section independently describes the same split (harness supplies the listing; the repo supplies the policy prose; the plugin supplies the tools).\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:1237; .../groom/finalize.md:269,308,328; .../hooks/hooks.json; .codex/hooks.json; harness_compatibility.json (accessed 2026-09-15)\\n  VERIFIED_BY: rg + Read + Bash on 2026-09-15\\n\\n---\\n\\n### C4 \u2014 `create/scope.md` forbids unlabelled causal claims and assigns cause verification to grooming\\n\\nCLAIM: \\\"`scope.md` forbids unlabelled causal claims at creation and assigns cause verification to grooming. A required 'layer that caused it' at creation time would compel the speculation that rule bans.\\\" (\u00a7 Open Question For Grooming)\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED \u2014 both halves are stated near-verbatim, and the constraint binds as the item describes\\n\\nEVIDENCE:\\n- Source: `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/scope.md`\\n- Retrieved: 2026-09-15\\n- Content: |\\n    :48  - a likely cause, labeled explicitly as `**Hypothesis**: {text}`, not stated as established fact\\n    :50  Not allowed at creation time:\\n    :55  - speculation about why the problem occurs, stated in the same declarative voice as an observed fact\\n    :59  Cause verification is grooming's job (`find-cause`, `dh:fact-checker`), not creation's \u2014 an\\n         unlabeled causal claim at creation time reaches the groomed item, and eventually the live\\n         issue, indistinguishable from a verified fact.\\n  Line 48 permits a cause only under the `**Hypothesis**:` label; line 55 bans the unlabelled form; line 59 assigns verification to grooming by name.\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n- Source: same file, lines 65-69 (the BEHAVIORAL/PROCESS rule) and lines 7-14 (Step 0 classification)\\n- Finding: line 69 extends the labelling requirement past the strip rule \u2014 \\\"Exception \u2014 hypothesis labeling still applies: if the procedural text contains a causal claim about why the behavior occurs that is not a confirmed observation, label it `**Hypothesis**: {text}` in place ... This applies to MIXED items as well.\\\" So no item class escapes the rule, including this item's own class. A mandatory cause-layer field at creation would therefore have to be satisfiable without a causal assertion for every classification, not just for product/feature items.\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The rule reads as the item states it, and the cross-check tightens rather than loosens it. The \u00a7 Open Question's inference is sound: a required \\\"layer that caused it\\\" at creation would demand a declarative causal claim, which line 55 bans and line 69 refuses to exempt \u2014 so the placement question the item raises is a real constraint, not a misreading. Note the path is `.../references/workflows/create/scope.md`; the item's shorthand `create/scope.md` resolves there (the discovery artifact gives the full path correctly).\\n\\nCITATION: |\\n  SOURCE: plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/scope.md:48,50,55,59,65-69 (accessed 2026-09-15)\\n  VERIFIED_BY: Read on 2026-09-15\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Summary\\n\\n| # | Claim | Verdict |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| C1a | `**Hypothesis**` 0 of 21 in one session | VERIFIED (0 of 24 as-filed; denominator 21 unconfirmed) |\\n| C1b | 15 of 21 wrote the other label unbolded | REFUTED as a number (19 of 23); direction understated |\\n| C1c | Session count vs Research section's 10/663 | VERIFIED; different population, revision, and regex |\\n| C2a | 19 rule pointers, 4 Codex events, #3446/#3437 timeline | VERIFIED |\\n| C2b | 63 `relevance_unanchored` entries | VERIFIED (63/331); single-entry fix INCONCLUSIVE |\\n| C2c | Security-upgrade bullet inverted, fixed in place | VERIFIED |\\n| C2d | First table's three rows | Row 3 VERIFIED; rows 1-2 structurally corroborated only |\\n| C3 | Code / prose-calling-tools / harness taxonomy | VERIFIED |\\n| C4 | `create/scope.md` bans unlabelled cause, defers to grooming | VERIFIED |\\n\\n**Load-bearing note for grooming.** C1a is only reproducible against creation-time issue revisions, not current bodies \u2014 17 of the items in the measured population were edited hours later to bold the label. Any success-threshold measurement this item's \u00a7 Research flags as MISSING (RT-ICA condition 10) must therefore specify which revision it counts, or it will measure the correction rather than the compliance. The re-runnable method is `gh api graphql ... userContentEdits(last:1){diff}`, not `gh issue view`.\\n\\n**Housekeeping.** \u00a7 Observed Failures carries entries `0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0` and `-1` with byte-identical content; the same duplication appears in \u00a7 The Required Behaviour, \u00a7 Why A Validated Field, \u00a7 Scope Note, and \u00a7 Open Question For Grooming. One of each pair is strikeable.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-14T19:47:15.853989Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: Backlog items record no layer analysis, so a defect gets fixed at the layer where it was noticed\\nDate: 2026-09-15\\nGoal: Know enough to plan an attribution that distinguishes the layer a defect was observed at from the layer that caused it, enforced by something other than prose.\\nConditions:\\n1. The layer taxonomy the attribution is made against | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Measured compliance of the existing prose-convention approach \u2014 0 of 21 for the comparable label | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. Whether `**Hypothesis**:` has a live reader that could carry an undetermined cause layer | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. Whether a warning-only signal is consumed by any downstream reader today \u2014 #2974 confirms it is not | Status: AVAILABLE\\n5. The item types in scope \u2014 the item states every plugin defect | Status: AVAILABLE\\n6. Whether the creation-stage schema can represent an undetermined value distinguishably from an omitted one | Status: DERIVABLE\\n7. Which lifecycle points validate item structure today, and what each does on failure | Status: DERIVABLE\\n8. Which consumers read a defect record and branch on its content | Status: DERIVABLE\\n9. How many existing open items would fail an attribution check | Status: DERIVABLE\\n10. The compliance rate that counts as success for this change, against the 0-of-21 baseline | Status: MISSING\\nAVAILABLE count: 5\\nDERIVABLE count: 4\\nMISSING count: 1\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-14T20:15:15.850886Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: recurring-pattern\\n\\n**Rationale**: Q1 is NO \u2014 the item carries unresolved design questions (three-state field shape, enforcement placement, whether a mandated field is filled in mechanically), so it is not a surface change. Q2 is YES and the tree stops there: the same problem class is recorded 6 times in the item across two sessions on separate dates, and 6 further occurrences were confirmed in closed history (#3139, #3095, #2970, #3169