eadable attribution becomes queryable through this without new code \u2014 which is the Functional Requirement 1 payoff \u2014 but only if the field reaches the item dict the filter sees. | Risk: LOW | Why: no change required; it is the evidence that \\\"readable as data\\\" has an existing consumer, and the compliance-rate measurement (NFR 1) can be run through it.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_view` - Lines 169-179 document `exclude_unset=True` as the fix for this exact class of problem on the response side, paired with `exclude_none=False`. A field that must transmit a load-bearing `None` has to override both, and `_respond`'s `exclude_none` default is `True` (line 148) \u2014 the opposite of what is needed. | Risk: HIGH | Why: the defaults pull in opposite directions; an attribution whose undetermined state is `None` is stripped from the MCP response by default, so every agent consumer reads it as absent.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::infer_type` - Lines 255-270, the only Python that branches on defect-record content, via keyword heuristic (`\\\"fix\\\"`, `\\\"bug\\\"`, `\\\"broken\\\"`, `\\\"vulnerability\\\"`). If the attribution is meant to influence typing, this is the existing branch point. | Risk: LOW | Why: isolated heuristic; not required to change, but it is the sole existing code-level branch on defect content and should be verified as unaffected.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/cli_inputs.py` - Lines 85-97 declare `issue_classification` and `analysis_method` with `AliasChoices` hyphen/underscore pairs. The CLI adapter is a full parallel surface to MCP; a new field added only to the MCP tool is unreachable from `backlog` CLI callers. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: two entry surfaces, one schema \u2014 a field added to one reaches only that surface's callers, which is the item's own thesis reproduced at the API boundary.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness