sk: MEDIUM\\n- `.github/workflows/quality-gate-audit.yml` | Role: ci | Connection: references backlog tooling | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `.dh/config.yaml` | Role: configuration | Connection: `backend.name: github` selects the backend with the silent parse path | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Migration / Replacement Coverage Matrix\\n\\nThis item is not a replacement, but it proposes superseding an existing prose convention (`**Hypothesis**:` plus the Issue Classification section) with a validated attribution. The coverage question is therefore live, and the Research section settled most of it.\\n\\n| Capability the current prose mechanism provides | Covered by a schema field? | Evidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Express a speculative cause at creation without asserting it | PARTIAL | `create/scope.md:48` permits `**Hypothesis**: {text}` as free text; a closed-domain enum cannot hold arbitrary prose, so the narrative half is not carried forward |\\n| Distinguish undetermined from omitted | MISSING | Research \u00a7RT-ICA #6: `str = \\\"\\\"` plus `yaml_io.py:125` collapse them on first persist; requires a serializer change, not just a field |\\n| Resolve the speculation during grooming and rewrite it in place | MISSING | `finalize.md:192-236` operates on description prose; no equivalent exists for a field, and the REFUTED branch has never executed repo-wide |\\n| Feed the claim to the fact-checker by exact text | MISSING | `swarm.md:193` matches claim text verbatim; a field value is not a claim string |\\n| Survive the `--quick` path | MISSING | `quick/start.md:139-164` strips `**Hypothesis**` deliberately; a field would need an explicit decision, currently absent |\\n| Be enumerable, filterable, countable across the backlog | COVERED (only by a field) | `backlog_list(filter_by_key=...)` already filters item dicts by key; this is the capability prose cannot provide and the reason for the change |\\n| Be validated against a closed value domain | COVERED (only by a field) | `_validate_add_item_type` (`operations.py:1398-1401`) refuses on `VALID_ITEM_TYPES` membership \u2014 the working pattern |\\n\\nEvery MISSING row is a HIGH migration risk: replacing the prose marker without carrying these forward removes a working (if rarely-fired) mechanism and leaves the gap uncovered.\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` - `layer` is already taken by the SDLC axis, the model's uniform `str = \\\"\\\"` shape cannot express the three states the item requires, and every backend, CLI, MCP, doc, and fixture reads from this one declaration.\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/finalize.md` + `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/references/groomer-output-validation.md` - Two unsynchronised copies of the required-sections table. A row added to one reaches only that skill's callers. This is the item's own failure mode already present inside the enforcement mechanism the item proposes to extend.\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/github_sync.py` - Unregistered content stored with no warning on the read path the active backend and the CI sync both use. Data present on disk that the next step never reads is the #2974 shape the item's own non-functional requirement 3 forbids reproducing.\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - compatibility \u2014 the `layer` name collision, and a field a hand-built metadata constructor drops on the sqlite path\\n  - behavioral \u2014 `exclude_none`/`exclude_unset` defaults pulling in opposite directions silently strip a load-bearing `None` from the MCP response\\n  - documentation \u2014 two lifecycle documents, a schema doc, and an ARCHITECTURE mirror all encode the current single meaning of `layer`\\n  - verification \u2014 no test covers omitted-vs-empty, no test covers the warn/silent asymmetry, no harness exists to measure compliance against the 0-of-21 baseline; three of five goals have no verification path\\n  - operational \u2014 CI syncs items unattended through the silent path and commits with `[skip ci]`\\n  - migration \u2014 five of seven current prose capabilities are MISSING under a field-only replacement\\n  - scope \u2014 the enforcement point is duplicated across two skills, three copies of the classification taxonomy exist, and four backends must each round-trip the field\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - Nine system groups beyond the item and artifact, enumerated in the `SCOPE_EXPANSION:` line above. The two most consequential for planning: `tests/test_section_name_registry_drift.py` is an existing, working, code-level gate whose caller set is the entire prose corpus \u2014 it is a direct candidate answer to \\\"enforced by something other than prose\\\" that the artifact's Design Question 6 does not consider; and `sam_schema/core/models.py`'s `IssueClassification` enum shows the same taxonomy already validated in code on the Task record, meaning the repo has already solved this problem once on a sibling record.\\n  - Fact-check and planning scope should both be widened accordingly. The artifact's Design Question 6 in particular is under-specified: it names \\\"the existing section-registry check\\\" as a single path, but that path has three halves with three different failure behaviours (refuse / warn-conditionally / silent), and a fourth enforcement mechanism (the drift test) that the question does not mention at all.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-14T20:01:39.457285Z</sub>\\n\\n### Research \u2014 backlog_core item schema + work-backlog-item create/groom gates \u2014 2026-09-15\\n\\nResearch method: 4-angle independent fan-out (api-state, ecosystem-research, impact-measurement, codebase-auditor). [CROSS-ANGLE] marks findings confirmed by 2+ independent angles. [CONFLICT] marks contradictions between sources. Every count below carries the command that produced it. All paths are relative to `plugins/development-harness/` unless absolute.\\n\\nScope note: `technology` here is an internal module, so the two external angles have short findings and long Gaps \u2014 expected, recorded, not a failure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Answers to the four DERIVABLE conditions\\n\\n#### RT-ICA #6 \u2014 Can the creation-stage schema represent *undetermined* distinguishably from *omitted*?\\n\\n**No. Not today, and not as a matter of convention \u2014 the distinction is destroyed by the persistence call itself.**\\n\\nEvery scalar on the creation-stage metadata model is `str` defaulted to `\\\"\\\"`:\\n\\n- `backlog_core/models.py:831` \u2014 `class BacklogItemMetadata(BaseModel)`\\n- `backlog_core/models.py:839-864` \u2014 all ~28 scalars declared `field: str = \\\"\\\"`, including `layer: str = \\\"\\\"  # SDLC Layer 0 (framework), 1 (language), 2 (stack)` at line 861\\n\\nThe one three-state type already in the item schema is `MilestoneInfo.number: int | None = None` (`models.py:826`). So `X | None` is available and used in this file \u2014 just not on any creation-stage field.\\n\\nPydantic's own unset-tracking exists and *would* carry the distinction, but the write path discards it. Verified empirically against the installed `pydantic 2.12.5`, re-runnable from the plugin root:\\n\\n```bash\\nuv run python - <<'EOF'\\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0, '.')\\nfrom backlog_core.models import BacklogItemMetadata, BacklogItem\\nomitted  = BacklogItemMetadata()\\nexplicit = BacklogItemMetadata(layer=\\\"\\\")\\nprint(\\\"in-memory fields_set:\\\", omitted.model_fields_set, \\\"vs\\\", explicit.model_fields_set)\\nd_om = BacklogItem(title=\\\"a\\\", metadata=omitted ).model_dump(exclude={\\\"file_path\\\",\\\"skip\\\"})\\nd_ex = BacklogItem(title=\\\"a\\\", metadata=explicit).model_dump(exclude={\\\"file_path\\\",\\\"skip\\\"})\\nprint(\\\"persisted dicts identical:\\\", d_om == d_ex)\\nprint(\\\"after round trip of OMITTED, layer in fields_set:\\\",\\n      \\\"layer\\\" in BacklogItemMetadata.model_validate(d_om[\\\"metadata\\\"]).model_fields_set)\\nEOF\\n```\\n\\nOutput:\\n\\n```text\\nin-memory fields_set: set() vs {'layer'}\\npersisted dicts identical: True\\nlayer present in persisted dict: True -> ''\\nafter round trip of OMITTED, layer in fields_set: True\\n```\\n\\n`model_fields_set` separates them in memory, for exactly as long as the object lives before its first write. `backlog_core/yaml_io.py:125` calls `item.model_dump(exclude={\\\"file_path\\\", \\\"skip\\\"})` with no `exclude_unset`, so every field materialises to its `\\\"\\\"` default; after the round trip **both** report `layer` as set. Omitted and explicitly-empty are the same byte on disk.\\n\\nThe repo already relies on that collapse as a deliberate signal, which is why fixing it by convention is not available: `operations.py:1633-1635` documents `item_ref` as \\\"its emptiness, not its absence, is the local-only-create signal\\\". Empty-string-means-absent is a load-bearing idiom here, not an oversight.\\n\\n**Name the type and field that would carry it.** There is no existing field to repurpose. `BacklogItemMetadata.layer` (`models.py:861`) is already occupied by the SDLC Layer 0/1/2 taxonomy and is a different axis from this item's code / prose / harness taxonomy \u2014 reusing the name collides. A new field would need to be declared `str | None = None` (the `MilestoneInfo.number` shape at `models.py:826`), or carry an explicit sentinel member in a `StrEnum` so `\\\"undetermined\\\"` is a value rather than an absence. `str | None` alone is not sufficient without a matching serializer change \u2014 `yaml_io.py:125` would have to stop flattening it.\\n\\n**Precedent that the mechanism works in this repo when wired deliberately**: `backlog_core/server.py:169-179` documents `exclude_unset=True` as the fix for exactly this class of problem for `backlog_view` (#3368) \u2014 \\\"keeps exactly the keys each branch put in *payload* (None or not) and drops the ~45 fields that branch never mentioned\\\". That precedent is on the *response* side only; nothing on the persistence side uses it.\\n\\n**Creation-stage surface is closed**: `operations.py:1603-1614`, `add_item()` accepts exactly `title, description, priority, source, type_, research_first, files, suggested_location, force, repo, output`. No free-form metadata pass-through. A new attribution cannot be smuggled through the existing signature.\\n\\n#### RT-ICA #7 \u2014 Which lifecycle points validate item structure, and what does each do on failure?\\n\\nFour distinct code behaviours, plus one prose gate. The behaviours are **not** uniform, and the non-uniformity is undocumented.\\n\\n| # | Point | File:line | On failure |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | `_validate_add_item_title` | `operations.py:1356-1375` | **Refuse** \u2014 `ValidationError`, before any storage or issue creation |\\n| 2 | `_validate_add_item_priority` | `operations.py:1378-1395` | **Refuse** \u2014 `ValidationError`; accepts `VALID_NEW_ITEM_PRIORITIES` plus any case-insensitive `idea*` |\\n| 3 | `_validate_add_item_type` | `operations.py:1398-1401` | **Refuse** \u2014 `ValidationError`; accepts `VALID_ITEM_TYPES` (`models.py:809`) or `ITEM_TYPE_ALIASES` (`models.py:810`) |\\n| 4a | `_normalize_section_key`, name == `\\\"Description\\\"` | `operations.py:978-984` | **Refuse** \u2014 `BacklogError`; the name is permanently unrecoverable by design |\\n| 4b | `_normalize_section_key`, unregistered name | `operations.py:1007-1014` \u2192 `_warn_unregistered_section` at `1017-1035` | **Warn and store** under `unknown__{key}`. stderr diagnostic unconditional; structured `Output.warnings` **only when the caller threads `output=`** |\\n| 5 | `github_sync.parse_issue_body`, unregistered `## Heading` | `github_sync.py:300-301` | **Silently store** under `heading_to_unknown_key(heading_name)`. No warning of any kind |\\n| 6 | Output Validation Gate (prose, agent-executed) | `skills/.../groom/finalize.md:100-168` | **Retry \u00d73 on the same model with a refined prompt, then set status `blocked`.** Not code. Table of required sections at `finalize.md:125-134` |\\n\\nDetail on #4b's conditionality \u2014 the warning reaches an MCP caller only on some paths:\\n\\n- `operations.py:1087` in `_write_groomed_to_item` (L1043) \u2014 passes `output=output` \u2705\\n- `operations.py:1294` in `_handle_batch_groomed` (L1262) \u2014 passes `output=out` \u2705\\n- `operations.py:1244` in `_handle_update_groomed` (L1222) \u2014 **no `output`**; stderr only\\n\\nScope-boundary checking in the groom gate is explicitly non-blocking: `finalize.md:181` \u2014 \\\"Scope violations do NOT block the write. Log violations as notes.\\\"\\n\\n**[CONFLICT] Code contradicts two in-repo documents on the \\\"identical behaviour\\\" claim.**\\n\\n- `section_registry.py:11-15` states: *\\\"Both the write path (`operations._normalize_section_key`, `github_sync._parse_groomed_section`) and the read path (`rendering.normalize_unknown_sections`) resolve names through the functions here, so a name is registered once and **behaves identically everywhere** it is read or written.\\\"*\\n- `operations.py:932-935` states the `unknown__` fallback *\\\"is reported (see `_warn_unregistered_section`) so a genuinely new, permanently unregistered section name is **visible immediately instead of silently accumulating** \u2014 the direct root cause #2970 exists to close.\\\"*\\n- The code disagrees. `github_sync.py:300-301` applies the same fallback with no warning. `github_sync.py` imports no `Output` and contains no warn call \u2014 verified: `rg -n \\\"Output|warn\\\" backlog_core/github_sync.py` returns nothing.\\n\\n**[RESOLVED]** Both documents describe *name resolution*, which is genuinely shared; neither describes *failure reporting*, which is not. The documents overstate. Consequence for this item: a section arriving by local write warns, and the identical section arriving by GitHub round trip does not \u2014 so the silent-accumulation failure #2970 exists to close remains open on the GitHub-parse path. This is directly load-bearing for the artifact's Design Question 6 (\\\"whether the enforcement point belongs in the same code path as the existing section-registry check\\\"): that code path has two halves with different behaviour, and only one of them is the \\\"known precedent\\\".\\n\\n#### RT-ICA #8 \u2014 Which consumers read a defect record and branch on its content?\\n\\n**In Python: essentially none.** `rg -n \\\"ROOT_CAUSE_ANALYSIS|ISSUE_CLASSIFICATION\\\" --glob '*.py' .` returns only declaration sites in `section_registry.py` (lines 74, 96, 107, 129, 150) \u2014 enum members and render-order entries. No code reads the *content* of `Issue Classification` or `Root-Cause Analysis` and branches on it.\\n\\nThe single piece of Python that branches on defect-record content is a keyword heuristic that *assigns* a type rather than consuming an attribution:\\n\\n- `parsing.py:255-270` \u2014 `infer_type(description, title)`; `if any(w in text for w in (\\\"fix\\\", \\\"bug\\\", \\\"broken\\\", \\\"vulnerability\\\")): return \\\"type:bug\\\"`\\n\\nEvery real consumer is a prose workflow read by an agent:\\n\\n| Consumer | Location | Branches on |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Groom swarm classifier | `groom/swarm.md:26-28`, `228-233` | item type == `defect` or `recurring-pattern` \u2192 runs Root-Cause Analysis 5-whys |\\n| Hypothesis Resolution | `groom/finalize.md:192-236` | `description` containing `**Hypothesis**: {text}`; precedence over `Root-Cause Analysis`'s `**Root cause**:` line, then `Fact-Check`'s `HYPOTHESIS:`-prefixed claim verdict (VERIFIED / REFUTED / INCONCLUSIVE) |\\n| Output Validation Gate | `groom/finalize.md:104-139` | presence of names in `sections_index` |\\n| Groomer agent | `agents/backlog-item-groomer.md:88` | `**Hypothesis**:` lines \u2014 \\\"treated as research questions\\\" |\\n| Fact-checker dispatch | `groom/swarm.md:15`, `193` | each `**Hypothesis**:` line becomes a claim, prefixed `claim:` / `HYPOTHESIS:` by exact text |\\n| Title strip rule | `create/start.md:83` | a causal clause in `title` \u2192 moved into `description` as `**Hypothesis**:` |\\n| Quick path | `quick/start.md:62`, `159` | templates `{hypothesis}`; `finalize.md:235-236` records that `--quick` never resolves it \u2014 \\\"Accepted scope limitation, not a bug\\\" |\\n\\n**Implication for the item's premise.** The item asks for \\\"a field the groom gate validates in code\\\". The groom gate is not code. `finalize.md` is prose executed by an agent, and its required-sections table (`finalize.md:125-134`) is a checklist the agent applies. Putting an attribution *field* on the schema and a *check* in `finalize.md` reproduces the enforcement topology that scored 0 of 21 \u2014 the check would live at the same layer as the rule that failed. The only lifecycle points that refuse in code today are `add_item`'s three argument validators (`operations.py:1356-1401`) and `_normalize_section_key`'s `\\\"Description\\\"` guard (`operations.py:978-984`).\\n\\n#### RT-ICA #9 \u2014 How many currently-open items would fail an attribution check?\\n\\n**663 of 663 \u2014 all of them \u2014 because no machine-readable attribution field exists to populate.** That number is trivially true and not useful on its own, so what follows is what was actually counted, with the commands.\\n\\n```bash\\ngh issue list -R Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills --state open --limit 2000 \\\\\\n  --json number,title,labels,body > open_issues.json\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\njq -r '\\n  \\\"total_open: \\\\(length)\\\",\\n  \\\"type histogram: \\\\([.[]|.labels[].name|select(startswith(\\\"type:\\\"))]|group_by(.)|map(\\\"\\\\(.[0])=\\\\(length)\\\")|join(\\\", \\\"))\\\",\\n  \\\"line-initial **Hypothesis**: markers: \\\\([.[]|select(.body!=null and (.body|test(\\\"(?m)^\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Hypothesis\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*:\\\")))]|length)\\\",\\n  \\\"  their numbers: \\\\([.[]|select(.body!=null and (.body|test(\\\"(?m)^\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Hypothesis\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*:\\\")))|.number]|join(\\\", \\\"))\\\",\\n  \\\"## Root-Cause Analysis heading: \\\\([.[]|select(.body!=null and (.body|test(\\\"(?m)^## Root-Cause Analysis\\\")))]|length)\\\",\\n  \\\"**Confirmed cause**: \\\\([.[]|select(.body!=null and (.body|test(\\\"\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Confirmed cause\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*\\\")))]|length)\\\",\\n  \\\"prose mentions an observed/cause layer: \\\\([.[]|select(.body!=null and (.body|test(\\\"(?i)layer observed|observed layer|cause layer|layer that caused\\\")))]|length)\\\"\\n' open_issues.json\\n```\\n\\n| Measure | Count | Denominator |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Open items | **663** | \u2014 |\\n| `type:bug` / `type:feature` / `type:refactor` / `type:chore` / `type:docs` | **171 / 240 / 71 / 60 / 37** | 663 (sums to 579; the remainder carry no `type:` label) |\\n| Carry a machine-readable observed-vs-cause layer attribution | **0** | 663 \u2014 no such field exists in `BacklogItemMetadata` |\\n| Mention an observed/cause layer anywhere in prose | **2** | 663 |\\n| Carry a line-initial `**Hypothesis**:` claim | **10** | 663 (1.5%) \u2014 #3454, #3448, #3436, #3424, #3328, #3326, #3325, #3322, #3305, #3298 |\\n| \u2026among `type:bug` only | **7** | 171 (4.1%) |\\n| Carry a `## Root-Cause Analysis` heading | **5** | 663; **2** of 171 `type:bug` |\\n| `type:bug` with either marker | **9** | 171 (5.3%) |\\n| `type:bug` labelled `status:groomed` | **31** | 171 |\\n| Carry `**Confirmed cause**` (a *resolved* hypothesis) | **0** | 663 open |\\n\\nCounting caveat, recorded so the number is not overstated: a naive `test(\\\"\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Hypothesis\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*\\\")` returns **12**, not 10. Two of those (#3453, #3451 \u2014 this item) only quote the marker inside backticks while *discussing* the convention. The `(?m)^\\\\*\\\\*Hypothesis\\\\*\\\\*:` anchor excludes them. Use the anchored form.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Settling the question the item raises and does not answer\\n\\n> Does the existing `**Hypothesis**:` marker already provide a working creation-time expression for an undetermined cause?\\n\\n**It has a live reader, and the reader has fired once in the repository's history. As a mechanism for carrying an undetermined value forward, it does not generalise.**\\n\\n**What reads it** \u2014 five prose readers, listed in RT-ICA #8 above. The strongest is `finalize.md:192-236`, which is a genuine three-way resolution machine: it rewrites `description` in place (not a side section \u2014 `finalize.md:200-201`, \\\"so the resolution is visible even to an agent reading just a truncated glimpse of the item\\\"), with a documented precedence order and a documented arity guard (`finalize.md:208-211`: the RCA tier applies only when exactly one hypothesis line exists, because RCA yields one root cause and multi-line attribution would be unreliable). This is a better-designed mechanism than the item credits it for.\\n\\n**Whether it works in practice** \u2014 measured, not assumed:\\n\\n```bash\\ngh search issues --repo Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills '\\\"**Confirmed cause**\\\"' --json number,title,state\\ngh search issues --repo Jamie-BitFlight/claude_skills '\\\"Hypothesis (refuted\\\"'  --json number --jq 'length'\\n```\\n\\n- `**Confirmed cause**` \u2014 **1** issue, repo-wide, open and closed: #3276 *\\\"fix: contract-verification agent's findings never reach the orchestrator under async subagent dispatch\\\"* (closed).\\n- `**Hypothesis (refuted \u2014 see Fact-Check section)**` \u2014 **0** issues, repo-wide.\\n- Highest issue number in the repo: **#3566**.\\n\\nSo across ~3,566 filed items the resolution path has produced one visible output and its REFUTED branch has never executed. 10 open items currently hold an unresolved marker.\\n\\n**Four structural reasons it does not generalise to a cause-layer attribution:**\\n\\n1. **It is not a value, it is a formatted line inside free text.** Every reader matches it by exact prose (`swarm.md:193` matches \\\"using the claim's own exact text\\\"; `finalize.md:212-215` requires the Fact-Check claim text to *exactly match* the line's text). Nothing can enumerate hypotheses, filter on them, or count them without re-parsing markdown. An attribution needs to be readable as data \u2014 Functional Requirement 1 in the discovery artifact.\\n2. **It cannot express a taxonomy.** `**Hypothesis**: {free text}` has no value domain. A layer attribution needs a closed set. The nearest working pattern in the repo is `_validate_add_item_type` (`operations.py:1398-1401`) checking membership in `VALID_ITEM_TYPES` \u2014 an enum validated at `add_item`, which refuses.\\n3. **Its \\\"undetermined\\\" state is the absence of the marker, which is the exact collapse RT-ICA #6 rules out.** An item with no `**Hypothesis**:` line is indistinguishable from an item whose author considered the cause and could not determine it. That is the same omitted-vs-undetermined failure, one layer up.\\n4. **It has a documented hole on the path most defects take.** `finalize.md:235-236` \u2014 `--quick` skips grooming entirely, so a hypothesis written on the quick path is never resolved. `--quick` is the routing AGENTS.md prescribes for \\\"a trivial single-file fix with an unambiguous cause\\\".\\n\\n**What *does* transfer.** Three design properties of the Hypothesis mechanism are worth carrying over regardless of the field's shape: the in-place rewrite rule (`finalize.md:200-201`, generalised at `finalize.md:238-249` as Canonical Write-Back, with #2498's three recorded failures as the evidence); the explicit arity guard for multi-valued resolution (`finalize.md:208-211`); and the named `--quick` scope exemption stated as an accepted limitation rather than left implicit.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### API State \u2014 Pydantic 2.12.5 (the one external surface in scope)\\n\\n**Current API, verified by execution rather than documentation** (command and output under RT-ICA #6):\\n\\n- `BaseModel.model_fields_set` \u2192 `set[str]`; contains only fields supplied to the constructor. Distinguishes unset from explicitly-default-valued **in memory**.\\n- `BaseModel.model_dump(exclude_unset=False)` (the default) materialises every field to its default, destroying that distinction. `yaml_io.py:125` uses the default.\\n- `BaseModel.model_dump(exclude_unset=True)` preserves it. Used in this repo at `server.py:2250, 2298, 2339, 2341, 2346` (response side only), documented at `server.py:169-179`.\\n- `X | None = None` and `exclude_none` are the alternative three-state carriers; `exclude_none` defaults to `True` in this repo's `_respond` helper (`server.py:148`), which is the opposite of what an undetermined-value field would need.\\n\\n**Changelog / breaking changes**: not retrieved. The Ref MCP tool exposed in this session (`Ref-local`) does not offer `ref_search_documentation`; the call returned `No such tool available`. No Pydantic changelog was fetched, and none of the above rests on one \u2014 every claim is from executing the installed version.\\n\\n**Gotcha**: `exclude_unset` and `exclude_none` are independent and the repo's defaults pull in opposite directions. `server.py:169-170` records that `backlog_view` is \\\"the one caller that needs it `True`, paired with `exclude_none=False`\\\" \u2014 a field that must transmit a load-bearing `None` has to override both.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Ecosystem and Compatibility\\n\\nNo external ecosystem research applies \u2014 `backlog_core` is an internal, unpublished module of this repository. Recorded rather than omitted, per the angle's constraints.\\n\\nThe internal analogue \u2014 how sibling items in this backlog report the same class of problem \u2014 is the useful substitute, and it is corroborated:\\n\\n- **#2974** (P0) \u2014 content stored under an `unknown__` key reads back as absent with `section_filter_miss: false`, i.e. a detection that produces no consumable signal. Referenced by this item's own RT-ICA condition 4 and by the discovery artifact's Non-Functional Requirement 3.\\n- **#2973** \u2014 description-embedded `##` headings promoted on a GitHub round trip; 136 of 663 open issues affected, measured 2026-09-15. The denominator **663** independently matches this research's live count (`gh issue list --state open --limit 2000 --json number --jq 'length'` \u2192 `663`), which cross-validates both measurements' population. [CROSS-ANGLE]\\n- **#2956 / #2970** \u2014 the incidents `section_registry.py` exists to prevent (module docstring lines 43-46).\\n- **#3446 / #3437** \u2014 the harness-hooks correction that was already on file when the prose fix was written; the artifact's sharpest evidence instance.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Quantitative Impact\\n\\n**Baseline** \u2014 all sizes from `wc -c`; token estimates are `chars \u00f7 3.5` (conservative) with `chars \u00f7 4` as the lower bound. All values are estimates.\\n\\n| Artifact | Size (chars) | Est. tokens (\u00f73.5) | Range bound (\u00f74) | Injected into agent context? |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| `backlog_core/operations.py` | 232,706 | 66,487 | 58,177 | No \u2014 code |\\n| `backlog_core/models.py` | 71,969 | 20,563 | 17,992 | No \u2014 code |\\n| `backlog_core/parsing.py` | 58,596 | 16,742 | 14,649 | No \u2014 code |\\n| `backlog_core/section_registry.py` | 10,979 | 3,137 | 2,745 | No \u2014 code |\\n| `skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` | 19,137 | 5,468 | 4,784 | Yes |\\n| `.../groom/finalize.md` | 17,775 | 5,079 | 4,444 | Yes \u2014 groom |\\n| `.../groom/swarm.md` | 11,313 | 3,232 | 2,828 | Yes \u2014 groom |\\n| `.../create/scope.md` | 3,985 | 1,139 | 996 | Yes \u2014 create |\\n\\nRegistry population, from `uv run python -c \\\"from backlog_core.section_registry import SectionKey, SubsectionKey; print(len(list(SectionKey)), len(list(SubsectionKey)))\\\"`: **28** `SectionKey` members, **24** `SubsectionKey` members.\\n\\n**Delta (pre-implementation estimates \u2014 the artifact does not exist yet; source measured is the item's own five-row table text at ~350 chars)**\\n\\n| Candidate placement | Size (chars) | Est. tokens (\u00f73.5) | Injection cost tier |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 5 `SectionKey` members + 5 `_SECTION_DISPLAY` rows | ~500 | ~143 | Negligible \u2014 and not injected; code |\\n| A `StrEnum` layer taxonomy + one `str \\\\| None` field on `BacklogItemMetadata` | ~600 | ~171 | Negligible \u2014 not injected; code |\\n| Five-row table instruction added to `create/scope.md` | ~350 | ~100 | Negligible |\\n| Same instruction also in `finalize.md` and `swarm.md` (3 injection sites) | ~1,050 | ~300 | Negligible |\\n\\n**Context Window Impact**: the largest plausible prose addition, ~300 tokens across three injection sites, is **0.9% of a 32k window and 0.23% of a 128k window**. No Blocking-risk artifact. Context cost is not a constraint on this decision in either direction \u2014 which cuts against \\\"prose is cheaper\\\" as an argument for the prose option, and equally against \\\"a schema field is expensive\\\".\\n\\n**Performance Impact**: No benchmarks found after searching `plugins/development-harness/benchmarks/` (does not exist) and `rg -l \\\"benchmark|pytest-benchmark|timeit\\\"` across `backlog_core/`, `docs/`, and `skills/work-backlog-item/` \u2014 no matches.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Codebase Audit\\n\\n`plugin-context: YES` \u2014 every target file is under `plugins/development-harness/`. Zip-and-move test applied: all findings concern code and prose *inside* the plugin bundle; nothing here proposes reaching outside it. The measurement commands (`gh`, `jq`) are research tooling, not shipped code.\\n\\nTools available: `rg` \u2705, `gh` \u2705, `jq` \u2705, `uv` \u2705. `graphify-graph` absent, `ccc` not initialised, `ast-grep` not used.\\n\\nSub-mode: **Behavioral Contract** (primary, for `_normalize_section_key` / `add_item` / `build_issue_body`), with **SKILL.md Flow** applied to `create/scope.md` \u2192 `groom/swarm.md` \u2192 `groom/finalize.md`.\\n\\n**Flow map \u2014 where an enforcement point could sit.** Adjacency, from the create and groom workflows:\\n\\n```text\\ncreate/start.md  --(strip causal clause from title -> **Hypothesis** in description, start.md:83)-->\\ncreate/scope.md  --(Step 0 classify: PRODUCT/FEATURE | BEHAVIORAL | MIXED, scope.md:7-14;\\n                    then creation-stage allow/deny lists, scope.md:30-59)-->\\noperations.add_item (title/priority/type validators REFUSE, operations.py:1356-1401)-->\\nparsing.build_issue_body (emits Description, Files?, Suggested Location?, Context;\\n                          deliberately emits NO Story and NO Acceptance Criteria, parsing.py:592-629)-->\\n       ... item filed ...\\ngroom/swarm.md   --(classifier -> type; if defect|recurring-pattern, RCA 5-whys, swarm.md:26-28, 228-233;\\n                    fact-checker takes each **Hypothesis** line as a claim, swarm.md:15, 193)-->\\ngroom/finalize.md--(RT-ICA Final Pass, finalize.md:14-98)-->\\n                 --(Output Validation Gate: required-section table, finalize.md:125-134;\\n                    retry x3 then status=blocked, finalize.md:156-168;\\n                    scope violations logged NOT blocked, finalize.md:181)-->\\n                 --(Hypothesis Resolution: rewrite description in place, finalize.md:192-236)-->\\n                 --(batch backlog_groom(sections=..., mark_groomed=True), finalize.md:258-269)\\n```\\n\\nThree insertion points exist, with materially different enforcement strength:\\n\\n- **Between `create/scope.md` and `build_issue_body`** \u2014 `add_item`'s validators. Refuses in code. Precedent: `_validate_add_item_type` (`operations.py:1398-1401`). Conflicts with `scope.md:55`'s ban on creation-time speculation *unless* the field's value domain includes an explicit undetermined member (see RT-ICA #6 \u2014 which the current `str = \\\"\\\"` schema cannot express).\\n- **`build_issue_body`'s deliberate omission** (`parsing.py:592-601`) \u2014 the artifact's Resolved-Questions table cites this as precedent for omitting a later-stage output, and the docstring is explicit about *why*: \\\"a template-filled section emitted at creation satisfies that check without anyone having written a criterion.\\\" That is the strongest in-repo statement of the failure mode the item's own Open Question names (\\\"a form that is substantially redundant is filled in mechanically\\\").\\n- **`finalize.md`'s required-section table** \u2014 prose, agent-executed, retry-then-block. Same enforcement layer as the rule that scored 0 of 21.\\n\\n**Behavioral contract \u2014 `_normalize_section_key(name, *, output=None) -> str`** (`operations.py:918-1014`)\\n\\n- Preconditions: `name` is a caller-supplied string; surrounding whitespace is stripped first (`operations.py:977`).\\n- Postconditions: returns a canonical `snake_case` key, or an `unknown__`-prefixed fallback key. Never returns an alias spelling and never re-emits a stored `unknown__` key whose name has since become canonical (`operations.py:988-1006`).\\n- Raises: `BacklogError` if `name.lower() == \\\"description\\\"` (`operations.py:978-984`) \u2014 the one section name that refuses. Rationale in docstring lines 962-975, found live on #2979/#2984/#2985.\\n- Edge case documented at `operations.py:1008-1011` (#3370): `heading_to_unknown_key` itself folds to a canonical key when the reconstructed heading is registered, so the warning is suppressed in that case \u2014 correctly, since the section *is* registered.\\n- **Undefined by static analysis**: whether a caller actually surfaces `Output.warnings` to a human or agent. `_warn_unregistered_section`'s own docstring (`operations.py:1020-1025`) concedes the stderr path exists precisely because \\\"no caller reads `output.warnings`\\\". #2974 records the consequence. Not determinable from the code alone \u2014 requires runtime observation of a specific consumer.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Cross-Angle Signals\\n\\n| Finding | Angles | Confidence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Omitted and empty are indistinguishable after the first persist \u2014 `str = \\\"\\\"` defaults plus `model_dump()` with no `exclude_unset` | codebase-auditor (`models.py:839-864`, `yaml_io.py:125`, `operations.py:1633-1635`) + api-state (executed against pydantic 2.12.5) | High |\\n| Detection without consumption is this system's established failure mode, not a hypothetical | codebase-auditor (`operations.py:1020-1025` docstring conceding no caller reads warnings) + ecosystem (#2974 filed P0 for the same shape) | High |\\n| A rule enforced only in prose under-fires by 1-2 orders of magnitude | impact-measurement (10/663 markers; 1 `**Confirmed cause**` across ~3,566 items) + codebase-auditor (`finalize.md`'s resolution machine exists entirely in prose, zero Python readers) | High |\\n| 663 is the correct open-item denominator | impact-measurement (live `gh issue list` count) + ecosystem (#2973's independent \\\"136 of 663\\\" measurement, same date) | High |\\n| The repo already knows template-filled fields defeat presence checks | codebase-auditor (`parsing.py:596-601`) + item's own Open Question (\\\"filled in mechanically\u2026 a skimmed instruction wearing a schema\\\") | High |\\n\\n### Conflicts\\n\\n- **[CONFLICT]** `section_registry.py:11-15` (\\\"behaves identically everywhere it is read or written\\\") and `operations.py:932-935` (\\\"visible immediately instead of silently accumulating\\\") **vs** `github_sync.py:300-301` applying the same fallback with no warning, in a module that imports no `Output` and contains no warn call. \u2014 **[RESOLVED]** Both docstrings describe name *resolution*, which is shared; neither describes failure *reporting*, which is not. The documents overstate. The silent-accumulation path #2970 exists to close is still open on the GitHub-parse half of that code path.\\n- **[CONFLICT]** Item body: \\\"`**Hypothesis**` appeared in **0 of 21**\\\" **vs** this research: 10 of 663 open items (1.5%), 7 of 171 open `type:bug` (4.1%). \u2014 **[RESOLVED]** Different populations, not a contradiction. The 0-of-21 was one session's filings; the repo-wide rate is non-zero but still low. The item's conclusion survives intact and is arguably strengthened: the resolution half of the mechanism has fired once in ~3,566 items.\\n- **[CONFLICT]** The item asks for \\\"a field the groom gate validates **in code**\\\" **vs** the groom gate being `finalize.md`, prose executed by an agent, with no Python reader of any groomed section's content. \u2014 **[RESOLVED]** Not a disagreement between sources; a gap between the item's assumption and the implementation. Recorded here because it changes what \\\"enforced by something other than prose\\\" can mean at that placement. Routes to the artifact's Design Question 2.\\n- **[CONFLICT]** The task brief described the discovery artifact as the file `plan/feature-context-layer-attribution.md` **vs** no such file existing (`find . -iname \\\"*layer-attribution*\\\"` \u2192 no matches; `git log --all --diff-filter=A` \u2192 never added). \u2014 **[RESOLVED]** `plan/feature-context-layer-attribution.md` is the artifact's *`artifact_id`*, not a path. `artifact list --item-id 3451` reports `storage_tier: \\\"remote\\\"`. Retrieved successfully via `uv run --script sam_schema/cli.py artifact read --item-id 3451 --artifact-type feature-context`. The path-shaped identifier is misleading but the artifact is intact and its Design Questions section was read in full.\\n\\n### Gaps (cross-angle)\\n\\n- **The success threshold (RT-ICA condition 10) is still MISSING and no angle could derive it.** The 0-of-21 baseline is a *rate on a session*; nothing in the repo defines what rate counts as success, nor a re-runnable measurement harness. The counting commands in RT-ICA #9 above are a candidate method \u2014 they are deterministic and re-runnable \u2014 but choosing the target number is a decision, not a finding.\\n- **Whether an `Output.warnings` entry ever reaches a human or agent is not determinable statically.** Both the code (`operations.py:1020-1025`) and the backlog (#2974) say it does not, but neither is a runtime observation of a specific consumer. Settling the artifact's Design Question 3 (\\\"a warning the caller already receives\\\") requires an actual runtime trace, which was out of scope here.\\n\\n### Gaps (single-angle)\\n\\n- **api-state**: no Pydantic changelog or release-notes retrieval. `mcp__Ref-local__ref_search_documentation` is not available in this session (`No such tool available`). No breaking-change analysis across Pydantic versions was performed. Every API claim rests on executing the installed 2.12.5 instead.\\n- **ecosystem-research**: no external sources exist for an internal module. GitHub issue search was scoped to this repository only. No search of upstream FastMCP, ruamel.yaml, or PyGithub issue trackers was performed \u2014 none is implicated by any finding above.\\n- **impact-measurement**: no benchmarks exist (searched `benchmarks/`, and `rg -l \\\"benchmark|pytest-benchmark|timeit\\\"` across `backlog_core/`, `docs/`, `skills/work-backlog-item/`). All delta figures are pre-implementation estimates measured against the item's own table text, not against a built artifact.\\n- **codebase-auditor**: `operations.py` is 5,505 lines; the audit read the section-registry path, `add_item`'s validators, the groomed-write handlers, and every `raise ValidationError` / `raise BacklogError` site, but not the file end-to-end. A validation point outside those paths could have been missed. `backlog_core/reconciliation.py` and `backlog_core/sync_engine.py` were not audited \u2014 if either validates item structure during a sync, it is not in the RT-ICA #7 table.\\n- **codebase-auditor**: the closed-issue population was queried only via `gh search issues` for two exact strings. A full closed-issue body corpus was not downloaded, so no closed-item attribution rate was computed.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Repo owner, session melodic-plotting-emerson, 2026-09-06\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-14T20:13:59.223406Z</sub>\\n\\n**Method**: 6-sigma frequency analysis via backlog history search (`gh issue list --state closed --limit 2000` + `jq` phrase match over bodies; the item's own six are excluded from the count below and treated as a separate, already-recorded population)\\n\\n**Frequency**: at least 12 occurrences \u2014 6 recorded in the item across two sessions, plus 6 independently confirmed in closed history spanning 2026-03 to 2026-09. The closed-history search is a floor, not a census: it matches phrasing, and this class has no field to query on, which is the item's own premise.\\n\\n**Matches (closed history, independent of the item's six)**:\\n- #3139 \u2014 Agent tools: frontmatter drifts from MCP server tool lists with no enforcement and silent failure in both directions \u2014 2026-08\\n- #3095 \u2014 docs/behavior mismatch: `map=True`'s \\\"always under 2,000 tokens\\\" claim is unenforced \u2014 2026-08\\n- #2970 \u2014 No canonical section-name registry \u2014 grooming agents hardcode arbitrary section names independently \u2014 2026-08\\n- #3169 \u2014 Duplicate detection matches title characters at write time, so it cannot tell a caller that the work already exists \u2014 2026-08 (same incident as the item's first-table row 1, counted once)\\n- #990 \u2014 Enforce complete-implementation quality gate phases with SAM tasks and hook checkpoints \u2014 2026-03\\n- #431 \u2014 Experiment protocol MCP server lacks content validation and enforcement \u2014 2026-03\\n\\n**Corroborating open items** (from this item's Research section, not double-counted above): #2974 (detection that produces no consumable signal), #2973 (136 of 663 items affected by a round-trip promotion), #3446/#3437 (the harness-hooks correction already on file before the prose fix was written).\\n\\n**Root cause**: A rule, correction, or check in this repo is placed at the layer where its symptom was observed rather than at the layer that can enforce it, because no record of the item ever states which layer produced the symptom and which layer caused it \u2014 so the observed layer is the only one available when the fix is written.\\n\\n**Measured expression of that cause**: 0 of 21 items in one session carried the `**Hypothesis**:` label `create/scope.md` mandates; 10 of 663 open items repo-wide (1.5%); `**Confirmed cause**` has been emitted once across ~3,566 filed items. A rule whose only enforcement layer is prose under-fires by one to two orders of magnitude.\\n\\n**Improvement**: A single enforcement point that refuses in code \u2014 the `add_item` validator tier at `operations.py:1356-1401`, which is the only lifecycle stage in this system that rejects rather than warns \u2014 carrying a closed value domain with an explicit `undetermined` member, would prevent all N; adding the same check to `finalize.md`'s required-section table would reproduce the exact enforcement topology that scored 0 of 21.\\n\\n**Fix locus**: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:831-864` (the field, which must be three-state \u2014 `yaml_io.py:125`'s unqualified `model_dump()` collapses omitted and empty to the same byte) and `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1398-1401` (the refusing validator, by precedent of `_validate_add_item_type`).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Observed Failures This Convention Would Have Surfaced\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nThree misdiagnoses in one session, each fixing at the layer where the symptom appeared:\\n\\n| Symptom observed | Layer worked | Layer it belonged to |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Duplicate check returned unrelated matches | term-selection code | prose instruction, and a code check that should not block |\\n| SubagentStop hook wrote another agent's task state | the hook's key lookup | the harness fact that transcripts are per-agent, and the prose step that registers at all |\\n| A prose label absent from every item | each item individually | a rule with no enforcement layer |\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nThree misdiagnoses in one session, each fixing at the layer where the symptom appeared:\\n\\n| Symptom observed | Layer worked | Layer it belonged to |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Duplicate check returned unrelated matches | term-selection code | prose instruction, and a code check that should not block |\\n| SubagentStop hook wrote another agent's task state | the hook's key lookup | the harness fact that transcripts are per-agent, and the prose step that registers at all |\\n| A prose label absent from every item | each item individually | a rule with no enforcement layer |\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-14T19:15:52.901634Z</sub>\\n\\nThree more, 2026-09-15, each caught by the repo owner rather than by the agent that made it:\\n\\n| Symptom observed | Layer worked | Layer it belonged to |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Non-Claude harnesses lost 19 rule pointers when `AGENTS.md` was culled | prose in `AGENTS.md`, telling those harnesses to go read the rule index | the hook wiring \u2014 `.codex/hooks.json` already runs four Codex events, and #3446 already recorded that both harnesses support plugin-bundled hooks |\\n| One research entry tripped `relevance_unanchored` | that entry, by adding an anchor to it | the 63 entries carrying the same warning, and the anchor being enforced at review rather than at write |\\n| A security-upgrade bullet read as an endorsement after a positive-framing rewrite inverted it | the bullet, reworded in place | the writing pass that can invert a prohibition, which has no gate re-checking the rewrite against its source |\\n\\nRow 1 tests row 5's sub-question directly. The prose fix reached only the agents that read the prose, and the correction was already on file in #3446 before the fix was written.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Why A Validated Field And Not A Documented Convention\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n`scope.md` names two labels \u2014 `**Hypothesis**:` and `**User-provided context**:`. Measured across the 21 items filed in one session: `**Hypothesis**` appeared in **0 of 21**, and 15 of 21 wrote the other label unbolded. A rule stated in prose with no enforcement at any layer produced zero compliance. A validator that refuses the item is the enforcement layer this rule currently lacks.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n`scope.md` names two labels \u2014 `**Hypothesis**:` and `**User-provided context**:`. Measured across the 21 items filed in one session: `**Hypothesis**` appeared in **0 of 21**, and 15 of 21 wrote the other label unbolded. A rule stated in prose with no enforcement at any layer produced zero compliance. A validator that refuses the item is the enforcement layer this rule currently lacks.\\n</div>\\n\\n## The Required Behaviour\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nEvery plugin defect starts with one five-row table:\\n\\n1. layer observed\\n2. layer that produced the artifact\\n3. layer that caused it\\n4. layer the fix targets\\n5. layer that will enforce the rule afterwards\\n\\nIn this repo that belongs in the backlog item schema as a field the groom gate validates in code, alongside the label fields whose compliance came out at zero.\\n\\nRow 5 carries a sub-question: **which callers traverse that layer?** A fix placed in prose covers only the callers that read the prose.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nEvery plugin defect starts with one five-row table:\\n\\n1. layer observed\\n2. layer that produced the artifact\\n3. layer that caused it\\n4. layer the fix targets\\n5. layer that will enforce the rule afterwards\\n\\nIn this repo that belongs in the backlog item schema as a field the groom gate validates in code, alongside the label fields whose compliance came out at zero.\\n\\nRow 5 carries a sub-question: **which callers traverse that layer?** A fix placed in prose covers only the callers that read the prose.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Open Question For Grooming\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n`scope.md` forbids unlabelled causal claims at creation and assigns cause verification to grooming. A required \\\"layer that caused it\\\" at creation time would compel the speculation that rule bans. Placement at the groom gate resolves it only if the creation-time schema accepts an unknown value.\\n\\nRows 1 and 2 coincide in most defects. A form that is substantially redundant is filled in mechanically, which is the failure mode of a skimmed instruction wearing a schema.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n`scope.md` forbids unlabelled causal claims at creation and assigns cause verification to grooming. A required \\\"layer that caused it\\\" at creation time would compel the speculation that rule bans. Placement at the groom gate resolves it only if the creation-time schema accepts an unknown value.\\n\\nRows 1 and 2 coincide in most defects. A form that is substantially redundant is filled in mechanically, which is the failure mode of a skimmed instruction wearing a schema.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Scope Note\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nA validator can enforce that five layers are named. It cannot enforce that the named layer is correct \u2014 the three failures above were made by someone capable of filling in a form. The gain is that a wrong answer becomes reviewable where an absent field is not.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the repo owner's framing \u2014 \\\"scope.md naming a label got 0 of 21; a validator refusing the item gets 21 of 21\\\", and \\\"a plugin is code and instructions as prose that sometimes use the tools created by that code, and both of them feed a harness that we also understand all parts of with its hooks and models and tool calling.\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nA validator can enforce that five layers are named. It cannot enforce that the named layer is correct \u2014 the three failures above were made by someone capable of filling in a form. The gain is that a wrong answer becomes reviewable where an absent field is not.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the repo owner's framing \u2014 \\\"scope.md naming a label got 0 of 21; a validator refusing the item gets 21 of 21\\\", and \\\"a plugin is code and instructions as prose that sometimes use the tools created by that code, and both of them feed a harness that we also understand all parts of with its hooks and models and tool calling.\\\"\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"14a75da28d013180f3cca70805755f590d4f210a0685b6f9e5f19d3ffa43adda\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABUflTaA\"}"}